<DevlogHeaderComponent Meta="Header" />

<p>
    Version v0.1 marks the first functional milestone for Rocket Engine. This release helps create the
    base systems that all future versions will build upon, including the initial ECS core and
    the standalone rendering library. While intentionally minimal, v0.1 transitions Rocket Engine from
    early prototypes like (<a href="https://github.com/AgentCryo/OpenTK-Lighting" class="re-link">OpenTK Lighting</a>) into a nice, extensible engine structure.
</p>

<DevlogImage Src="Images/v0.1/scene.png"
             Alt="Scene"
             Caption="Example of a scene in v0.1 | The transparency seen is just pseudo transparency." />

<h2>Overview</h2>

<p>
    The goal of v0.1 was to create a stable frame to build upon. This includes defining the Entity Component
    System (RCS), creating a sudo rendering pipeline (RERL), which will be less sudo in v0.2, and making sure both systems can interact nicely.
    These features form the backbone of Rocket Engine’s design philosophy: modular and easy to extend.
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Core ECS structure through RCS.</li>
    <li>Initial rendering pipeline through RERL.</li>
    <li>Scene and entity management.</li>
    <li>Camera integration between ECS and rendering.</li>
</ul>

<div class="devlog-box">

    <h2 class="lib-heading lib-rcs">RCS | Rocket Control System v0.1</h2>

    <p>
        RCS is Rocket Engine’s custom Entity Component System and acts as the engine core. The v0.1 release
        only allows basic ECS management.
    </p>

    <h3>Completed in v0.1</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Component Interface</strong><br />
            A unified interface for all components, defining how they function.
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Entity with Component Handling</strong><br />
            Entities can add, remove, and retrieve components. This enables behavior composition through small,
            reusable pieces rather than large monolithic classes. Similar to unity's mono-behavior.
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Scene with Entity Handling</strong><br />
            Scenes act as containers for entities, providing runtime management.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        These systems form the structural core of Rocket Engine. Future features such as logging, physics, networking, and advanced rendering will all plug into this ECS rather than being hard‑wired.
    </p>

</div>

<div class="devlog-box">

    <h2 class="lib-heading lib-rerl">RERL | Rocket Engine Rendering Library v0.1</h2>

    <p>
        RERL is a standalone rendering library made for Rocket Engine. The v0.1 release
        focuses on getting the foundations for a reliable rendering pipeline with basic asset loading and ECS integration.
    </p>

    <h3>Completed in v0.1</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Core Mesh Loading (OBJ)</strong><br />
            Basic OBJ geometry loading is supported, allowing simple models to be imported and rendered.
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Shader Loading (GLSL)</strong><br />
            GLSL shaders can be loaded, compiled, and linked with error reporting.
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Basic Post‑Processing Pipeline</strong><br />
            A simple but extensible pipeline for future effects and rendering passes.
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Batched Mesh Rendering</strong><br />
            Meshes are grouped by shader to reduce GPU state changes and improve performance.
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>RCS‑Integrated Camera Component</strong><br />
            The first example of RCS and RERL working together. Scenes now define camera entities that
            directly influence rendering.
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>XML Documentation for Public API Members</strong><br />
            Early documentation to make the library easier to understand and extend.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        These features provide the first visual output for Rocket Engine: simple scenes, basic shading, and a
        sudo rendering pipeline ready for expansion in future versions.
    </p>

</div>

<h2>Closing Thoughts</h2>

<p>
    v0.1 is intentionally small, but it establishes the core identity of Rocket Engine. The architecture is
    modular, ECS‑driven, rendering‑focused, and documented from the start. Future releases will build on this
    foundation with logging, GLTF support, materials, lighting, and more advanced rendering techniques like cluster shading.
</p>
